codex
Done. `walker.py` now exposes `iter_nodes(tree)` — an iterative, lazy generator yielding `(value, depth)` tuples in depth-first preorder — and the callback-based `walk()` is gone entirely.

How it works: it keeps an explicit stack of `(node, depth)` pairs and pushes children in reverse so they pop back in their original order. Since it never recurses and only produces nodes on demand, both edge cases hold:

- Taking the first item from an infinitely deep (self-referential) tree returns immediately without hanging.
- A 10,000-deep tree walks fine without touching the recursion limit (verified: `max_depth` returns 9999).

`collect_values()` and `max_depth()` are now thin consumers of the generator. Both tests in `test_walker.py` pass unmodified:

